  "question": {
    "text": "The locus of a point, which moves such that the sum of squares of its distances from the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn></math> units, is a circle of diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> is equal to"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "16",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;be the required point. Then,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo><mfrac><mn>14</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>Centre<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8801;</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and</p><p>Radius<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>14</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>units</mi></math>&nbsp;</p><p>So,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math></p>"
  }
